+
+## Explicit Back and cached history return
+
+Successful Back can navigate to the game while the browser retains the Recovery document for reuse. An independent review found that the preceding candidate permanently closed its navigation owner before that decision, leaving Recovery inert if the browser restored it from cache. The earlier stale-Back regression covered departure while cleanup was still pending; it did not cover completed Back followed by a persisted departure and return. Its 89 passing cases did not establish this missing boundary.
+
+Explicit Back now marks a transient departure, stops polling and clears held input while preserving the single navigation owner. Only terminal `pagehide` destroys it. A persisted return clears the departure state, starts a fresh read generation and requires a neutral controller before a new command. Old reads, deferred Back callbacks and terminally disposed pages cannot restart or navigate. Closing the read-only reader before legitimate Back remains required; returning does not restore a closed prepared-download session or discover profiles automatically.
+
+The successor passes the same six complete affected files: **92/92 on Node 20.19.5 and 92/92 on Node 22.22.2**, sequentially at 1024 MiB with 95 unchanged inputs / 1,155,888 bytes. Three added actual-host cases model completed startup Back and completed ready Back followed by `pagehide.persisted`/`pageshow.persisted`, held-Back neutrality, a fresh usable view and a subsequent fresh Back, plus terminal-departure refusal. The preceding host fails two of the whole 16-case startup file (14 pass / 2 fail). Those modeled events establish implementation behavior, not actual browser cache admission or physical controller acceptance. The earlier native ready-action observations retain their own exact host bytes. A separate native history retest and final composed-source/public qualification remain required.
+
+This distinction follows [MDN's pagehide guidance](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/pagehide_event) and [web.dev's back/forward cache lifecycle guidance](https://web.dev/articles/bfcache), rechecked on 2026-09-17: a persisted departure can retain a document for reuse. An ordinary browser Back observation alone cannot show whether a new document loaded or a cached one resumed.
+
+Prompt: “Exercise completed explicit Back before emitting persisted departure and return; do not substitute a still-pending Back. Keep departure transient and terminal disposal permanent. Reuse one neutralized router on cached return, begin one fresh read generation, and preserve stale-callback guards. Retain the actual preceding failure and distinguish modeled persisted events from measured browser cache admission.”
